From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Bongaarts <cab@bongalow.net>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 19:26:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] 9717: Bound a jump table by its own lowest target (#9447)

A table cannot extend past the first address it dispatches to: the table
bytes and the code they target cannot occupy the same memory. Walk the
entries in order tracking the lowest target seen so far, and cut the table
off at the first entry whose own table bytes would reach it.

This is the only bound available when the switch index is unconstrained at
the site, where the entry count is an invariant of the caller. That is the
normal situation for a doubled byte index on an 8-bit architecture, and the
case the 1-byte switch-variable guard in findSmallestNormal misses once the
range has been divided by the stride (#9447): an unbounded byte index read
as a 128-case switch, recovering 256 bytes of "table".

It runs inside JumpBasic::sanityCheck, which already exists to cut a table
off at the first unreasonable address and already owns the truncation; its
existing |diff| > 0xffff test can never fire in a 16-bit space. It runs
before LoadTable::collapseTable, so loadpoints still hold one entry per LOAD
in execution order and loadcounts maps entries to their LOADs. Each entry's
byte extent therefore comes from the LOADs it actually performed, with no
assumption about stride or contiguity, and LOADs at a fixed address (a
lookup rather than a table) are ignored. LoadTable gains read accessors for
this.

The bound only ever shrinks a table, never rejects a model, and only runs
when per-entry LOADs were collected. It declines unless the table lies below
its lowest target, so a table stored above or away from the code it
dispatches to (the usual .rodata arrangement) is untouched.
